  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 1254 PM MDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nTuesday through Thursday, not much change in the upper pattern is \nforecast with the upper ridge over the Southeastern United States \nwith an upper trough over the Northwest United States. This is \nforecast to keep the air mass roughly the same each day and allow \nfor highs in the 90s and low 100s. The warmer temperatures favor \nareas that are closer to Central Kansas. Winds should remain around \n5-15 mph and vary in direction each day as broad low pressure is \nexpected to linger over much of the Plains. With the upper level \nsouthwest flow and upstream trough, mid to high level moisture is \nforecast to continue streaming into the area and bring in high level \ncloud cover. A few shortwaves should also move through and help \nspark storm development west of the area. With the low levels \nforecast to be a bit on the dry side, storms will likely struggle to \nmake it to the area in the absence of outflows or a low level jet. \nStill, each day will give the area a chance for storms and maybe \neven some severe weather.\n\nGoing into the weekend, confidence has increased a bit more on the \nridge amplifying over the Plains. This is keeping the pattern \nroughly the same and conditions somewhat similar. Temperatures may \nbe able to warm into the 100s for most of the area if the ridge axis \nshifts over the area. Otherwise, we should keep the light winds and \ndaily chances for storms.\n\nThere is still a 25% though that the upper trough pushes east over \nthe weekend instead. In this case, Friday/Saturday would be hot and \nhave better chances for storms as the trough pushes a more pronounce \nsystem through the area. The rest of the weekend would then probably \nbe cooler with highs maybe in the 80s. Storm chances would also \nprobably be a bit lower on Sunday as well.",
